.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-CODECS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-codecs
.Nd fetch the patent-encumbered media codecs from RPM Fusion, on request
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
kldload ships only open source and does not redistribute codecs such as
H.264, HEVC, AAC or MP3.
.Nm
fetches them onto this machine for whoever runs it: it asks first, turns
on the RPM Fusion free and nonfree repositories for this release of
Fedora or of the RHEL family, installs ffmpeg, the GStreamer plugins, lame
and VLC, and then checks that ffmpeg can decode H.264, HEVC and AAC.
It needs root and the internet.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the header comment and exit 0.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when the decoders are there, or it was not confirmed; 1 when it is not
root, the distribution is not Fedora or the RHEL family, a package did not
install, or a decoder is missing.
.Sh EXAMPLES
In a Fedora 44 container, answering
.Ql y ;
the package manager's output is left out:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
==> Enabling RPM Fusion for fedora 44...
==> Installing codecs (full ffmpeg + GStreamer + VLC)...
==> Verifying H.264/HEVC/AAC decoders...
   h264: ok
   hevc: ok
   aac: ok
Done. Every app (browsers, players, VLC) can now decode common media.
.Ed
.Pp
In a Debian 13 container:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-codecs
kldload-codecs: RPM Fusion is for Fedora and the RHEL family; this is debian
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dnf 8
.Sh HISTORY
On 2026-09-30, writing this page: it asked RPM Fusion for the RHEL
family's package on every system, which on Fedora does not exist, so it
could not run on kldload's main distribution.
